perial ambition and its willingness to intervene in the affairs of other Greek city-states, setting a dangerous precedent for the wider Hellenic world.

At the Battle of Sybota in 433 BCE, Lacedaemonius was entrusted with a squadron of ten Athenian triremes, with strict instructions from Pericles not to engage the Corinthians unless they attempted a landing on Corcyra itself. Despite these orders, the initial Athenian force found itself drawn into the chaotic naval engagement, intervening to prevent the total rout of the Corcyraean fleet. The arrival of a second, larger Athenian fleet under Glaucon and Andocides ultimately forced the Corinthians to retreat, but Lacedaemonius's earlier, more restrained involvement highlighted the delicate balance Athens sought to maintain between deterrence and open warfare, a balance that ultimately failed.

The strategic consequences of Lacedaemonius's command at Sybota were profound, directly accelerating the onset of the Peloponnesian War. The Athenian intervention, regardless of its initial limited scope, was perceived by Corinth as a blatant act of aggression and a violation of the Thirty Years' Peace, fueling their grievances and intensifying their calls for Spartan intervention. While Lacedaemonius himself faded from the historical record after this event, the incident he participated in served as a crucial casus belli, solidifying the alliances and antagonisms that would define the next three decades of Greek history."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-15T21:04:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29448222964", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["Athens' growing naval power and imperial ambitions directly led to its willingness to intervene in Corcyraean affairs.", "Escalating tensions between Corinth and Corcyra created a critical flashpoint that drew in Athenian military support.", "Lacedaemonius's membership in the powerful Philaid clan secured him a prominent military command in a crucial diplomatic mission."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-15T21:04:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29448222964", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "500 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "500 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The Battle of Sybota, involving Lacedaemonius's fleet, further inflamed Corinthian resentment, directly contributing to the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War.", "His command demonstrated At